From 7a8e493844cd13f1f93fe8073961220879cc489c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Viorel Cernateanu <vrilutza@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 30 Jul 2026 23:45:02 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] spa: v4l2: do not walk past the buffers the caller provided

spa_v4l2_use_buffers() iterates up to reqbuf.count over buffers[], which is the
array of n_buffers entries the caller passed in, so b->outbuf = buffers[i] reads
past the end of it whenever VIDIOC_REQBUFS hands out more buffers than were
asked for. port->n_buffers is then set to that larger count as well, so
everything downstream of it keeps looking at entries that were never provided.
port_use_buffers() rejects n_buffers > MAX_BUFFERS, but nothing bounds
reqbuf.count, so a driver returning more than MAX_BUFFERS would walk past
port->buffers[] too.

Getting more than requested is documented behaviour, not a driver bug:

  "the driver will attempt to allocate the requested number of buffers and it
   stores the actual number allocated in the count field. It can be smaller
   than the number requested, even zero, when the driver runs out of free
   memory. A larger number is also possible when the driver requires more
   buffers to function correctly."
                    -- Documentation/userspace-api/media/v4l/vidioc-reqbufs.rst

And it is reachable rather than theoretical. The range this plugin advertises
for SPA_PARAM_Buffers is RANGE(SPA_MIN(4, max_buffers), 1, max_buffers), so a
peer can negotiate fewer than four. On a MacBookPro14,1 with the facetimehd
driver, VIDIOC_REQBUFS returns four buffers for any request below four, at every
resolution the sensor offers:

	requested   granted
	        1         4
	        2         4
	        3         4
	        4         4

Use the count the caller gave us, and log the discrepancy at warn so it is
visible on devices where it happens. The extra buffers are left alone, which is
the best that can be done here: the caller owns the array and there is nowhere
to put them.

Note what this does not claim to fix: if the driver needed those extra buffers
to function, using fewer may not work well, as the same paragraph of the
documentation implies. That is a separate question from reading outside the
array.

Tested on the machine above. This exact path is not exercised there -- the
device supports EXPBUF, so PipeWire takes the allocation path through
mmap_init(), whose loop is already bounded by n_buffers -- so the fix itself is
from reading the code; only the driver behaviour that triggers it is measured.

diff --git a/spa/plugins/v4l2/v4l2-utils.c b/spa/plugins/v4l2/v4l2-utils.c
index df5b0e185..ad6ddace5 100644
--- a/spa/plugins/v4l2/v4l2-utils.c
+++ b/spa/plugins/v4l2/v4l2-utils.c
@@ -1815,8 +1815,14 @@ static int spa_v4l2_use_buffers(struct impl *this, struct spa_buffer **buffers,
 				this->props.device, reqbuf.count, n_buffers);
 		return -ENOMEM;
 	}
+	if (reqbuf.count > n_buffers) {
+		/* The driver is allowed to hand out more buffers than asked for, but we
+		 * only have n_buffers of them to attach the extra ones to. */
+		spa_log_warn(this->log, "'%s' provided %d buffers, using %d",
+				this->props.device, reqbuf.count, n_buffers);
+	}
 
-	for (i = 0; i < reqbuf.count; i++) {
+	for (i = 0; i < n_buffers; i++) {
 		struct buffer *b;
 
 		b = &port->buffers[i];
@@ -1889,7 +1895,7 @@ static int spa_v4l2_use_buffers(struct impl *this, struct spa_buffer **buffers,
 
 		spa_v4l2_buffer_recycle(this, i);
 	}
-	port->n_buffers = reqbuf.count;
+	port->n_buffers = n_buffers;
 
 	return 0;
 }

